%[text] Student version
%[text] These exercises accompany the Optical Communication Pulse Trains active script. They focus on sampled data sequences, additive Gaussian receiver noise, first-order electrical filtering, and eye diagrams.
%[text] Complete the TODO blocks in each work area. The companion solution file is Week11\_optical\_communication\_pulse\_trains\_solutions.m.
%[text] Setup — clear removes workspace variables, close all closes prior figures, clc clears the Command Window, and format compact reduces printed whitespace. The groot settings set readable default font sizes for the plots.
clear
close all
clc
format compact
set(groot,'DefaultAxesFontSize',14,'DefaultTextFontSize',14, ...
    'DefaultLegendFontSize',11,'DefaultColorbarFontSize',14);
%%
%[text] ## Exercise 1: NRZ Optical Power Waveform
%[text] A baseband optical communication simulation usually represents the slowly varying optical power envelope rather than the optical carrier oscillation itself. For this exercise, use a wavelength of 1550 nm as the system context, but simulate the normalized optical power envelope.
%[text] Use a bit rate of `40 Gb/s`, a `48` bit pseudorandom sequence, and `128` samples per bit. Since `1 Gb/s` has the same numerical period as `1 GHz`, the bit period in picoseconds is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $T\_b={1000\\over R\_b},$
%[text] when $R\_b$ is in Gb/s and $T\_b$ is in ps. Use `round(rand(\[1,48\]))` or a similar command to generate the binary data.
%[text] Tasks:
%[text] 1\. Convert the bit rate to a bit period in ps.
%[text] 2\. Create a uniformly sampled time vector for all 48 bits.
%[text] 3\. Generate an NRZ waveform where each bit slot is completely filled by either the zero level or the one level.
%[text] 4\. Plot the first 8 bits of the NRZ waveform.
%[text] ## Exercise 1 Work Area
%[text] TODO: Add your code for Exercise 1 here.
%[text] ## Worked solution 1
%[text] Step 1 — Specify the physical context and sampling. The wavelength labels the optical system; this baseband calculation models normalized power, so it does not resolve the optical carrier. NRZ means non-return-to-zero. The waveform uses one-level power 1 and zero-level power 0, holding each level for the full bit slot.
wavelength_nm = 1550 ;  % wavelength in nm 
bit_rate_Gbps = 40 ;
number_of_bits = 48 ;  % total number of bits in waveform
samples_per_bit =  128; 

%[text] Step 2 — Convert units and construct the grid. At 40 Gb/s, each bit lasts 25 ps. Dividing by 128 gives dt = 0.1953125 ps. There are 48 × 128 = 6144 samples. Starting sample numbers at zero places the first sample at t = 0; ending at N−1 avoids duplicating the endpoint of the 1200 ps record.
bit_period_ps = 1000/bit_rate_Gbps ;
dt_ps = bit_period_ps/samples_per_bit ;
time_ps = (0:number_of_bits*samples_per_bit-1)*dt_ps ;
%[text] Step 3 — Generate repeatable random data and form the NRZ waveform. rng fixes the random-number seed so a rerun gives the same example. rand generates uniform numbers, and round maps them to equally probable zeros and ones. The make\_nrz\_waveform helper holds each bit value for its entire slot. Consecutive one bits therefore form an uninterrupted high level; consecutive zero bits form an uninterrupted low level.
% rng(2129)  % Optional -- fixing the random number seed guarantees the
% same waveform every time
bits = round(rand(1,number_of_bits)) ;  % create a random sequence of bits
bit_period_ps = 1000/bit_rate_Gbps;  % width (in ps) of every bit 
% % floor maps time to a zero-based bit slot; +1 converts to MATLAB indexing.
bit_index = floor(time_ps/bit_period_ps) + 1;
% % Guard against time samples outside the supplied bit sequence.
valid = bit_index >= 1 & bit_index <= numel(bits);
nrz_power = zeros(size(time_ps));
% % Repeat each bit value at every sample assigned to its slot.
nrz_power(valid) =  bits(bit_index(valid))  ;

%[text] Step 4 — Select the display interval without shortening the simulation. The logical mask selects t \< 200 ps, the first eight bit slots. 
first_bits_to_show = 8;
display_mask = time_ps < first_bits_to_show*bit_period_ps;
%[text] Step 5 — Inspect the NRZ waveform. stairs emphasizes the piecewise-constant samples, and dotted vertical lines mark bit boundaries. The vertical limits leave space around the zero and one levels. Transitions occur only when adjacent bits differ; the signal stays high across consecutive one bits.
figure('Name','Solution 1: NRZ waveform') %[output:73923d2c]
plot(time_ps(display_mask),nrz_power(display_mask),'k-', ... %[output:73923d2c]
     'LineWidth',1.4) %[output:73923d2c]
% 
grid on %[output:73923d2c]
xlabel('time (ps)','FontSize',14) %[output:73923d2c]
ylabel('power','FontSize',14) %[output:73923d2c]
ylim([-0.05 1.15]) %[output:73923d2c]
title('NRZ waveform: each bit fills the full bit slot'); %[output:73923d2c]
%[text] Step 6 — Report the parameters and transmitted sequence. table labels each quantity with its units. Converting bits to strings and joining them produces a readable 48-character sequence. Omitting the final semicolon displays the table in the Live Editor.
waveform_summary = table(wavelength_nm,bit_rate_Gbps,bit_period_ps,dt_ps, ... %[output:group:81591fb6] %[output:639d89d7]
    number_of_bits,samples_per_bit, ... %[output:639d89d7]
    string(join(string(bits),'')), ... %[output:639d89d7]
    'VariableNames',{'Wavelength_nm','BitRate_Gbps','BitPeriod_ps', ... %[output:639d89d7]
    'SampleSpacing_ps','NumberOfBits','SamplesPerBit', ... %[output:639d89d7]
    'BitSequence'}) %[output:group:81591fb6] %[output:639d89d7]
%[text] ## Exercise 2: Additive Gaussian Noise and Decisions
%[text] A simple receiver model adds Gaussian noise to the normalized power trace:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_{\\it{meas}}(t)=P(t)+\\sigma n(t),$
%[text] where $n(t)$ is a zero-mean random sequence with unit standard deviation. Use the NRZ waveform from Exercise 1, noise RMS `sigma = 0.18`, and a decision threshold of `0.5`. Sample the waveform once per bit at the bit center.
%[text] For equal zero and one levels at 0 and 1, equal Gaussian noise on both levels, and a threshold halfway between them, the approximate analytical bit-error probability is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_e=Q\\!\\left({0.5\\over \\sigma}\\right)\n ={1\\over 2}\\it{erfc}\\!\\left({0.5\\over \\sqrt{2}\\sigma}\\right).$
%[text] Tasks:
%[text] 1\. Add Gaussian noise to the NRZ waveform.
%[text] 2\. Sample the noisy waveform at the center of each bit.
%[text] 3\.  Plot the clean waveform, noisy waveform, and bit-center samples for the first 8 bits.
%[text] ## Exercise 2 Work Area
%[text] TODO: Add your code for Exercise 2 here.
%[text] ## Worked solution 2
%[text] Step 1 — Set the receiver model. The noise standard deviation is 0.18 in normalized power units, and the decision threshold is halfway between 0 and 1. Run the script from the top: this exercise reuses the NRZ waveform and sampling grid from Exercise 1.
noise_rms =   0.18  ;
decision_threshold = 0.5  ;
%[text] Step 2 — Add noise, sample, and decide. randn(size(nrz\_power)) produces one independent unit-variance Gaussian noise sample per waveform sample; multiplying by noise\_rms sets its standard deviation. The seed makes this example reproducible. Negative measured values are allowed because the model represents an additive receiver signal, not a constraint on physical optical power. 
% rng(2130)
noisy_nrz_power = nrz_power + noise_rms*randn(size(nrz_power));

%[text] Step 3 — Inspect the first eight decisions. plot shows the noisy trace, stairs overlays the clean reference, and circular markers show only the samples actually used by the detector. hold on retains the existing curves. The horizontal line is the threshold; padded limits keep noise excursions visible. A sample above the threshold is classified as one, regardless of the waveform between sampling instants.
figure %[output:78ccda7c]
hold on %[output:78ccda7c]
plot(time_ps(display_mask),nrz_power(display_mask), '--r','LineWidth',1.4) %[output:78ccda7c]
plot(time_ps(display_mask),noisy_nrz_power(display_mask), ... %[output:78ccda7c]
    'Color',[0.55 0.55 0.55],'LineWidth',1.0) %[output:78ccda7c]
hold off %[output:78ccda7c]
grid on %[output:78ccda7c]
xlabel('time (ps)','FontSize',14) %[output:78ccda7c]
ylabel('normalized power','FontSize',14) %[output:78ccda7c]
% 
legend('noisy NRZ trace','noise-free NRZ reference') %[output:78ccda7c]
title(sprintf(['Noisy NRZ trace, sigma = %.2f, threshold = ', ... %[output:group:9cc77885] %[output:78ccda7c]
    '%.2f'],noise_rms,decision_threshold)); %[output:group:9cc77885] %[output:78ccda7c]
%[text] 

